  "summary": "A short-lived but influential European avant-garde movement known for its spontaneous, childlike imagery and vibrant colors.",
  "description": "Characterized by bold, expressive brushwork, distorted figures, and mythological or primitive themes, rejecting both geometric abstraction and Surrealism's intellectualism. Formed by artists from Denmark, Belgium, and the Netherlands, reflecting a desire for renewal after WWII through raw, immediate expression.",
  "culturalContext": "CoBrA (1948-51) — Copenhagen-Brussels-Amsterdam's avant-garde (Asger Jorn, Karel Appel, Constant, Corneille, Dotremont) — painted spontaneous child-and-myth energy against Cold War rationalism: thick impasto beasts, scribble-faces, \"experiment\" collective wall-painting (Stedelijk 1949), and the journal Cobra's polemics. Dissolving after three years, it fed Jorn's Situationist International, Danish abstract tradition, and every \"child-art as high art\" argument since.",
